WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:04.650
Our bodies are capable of a huge range of tasks,

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:06.170
from the ordinary breathing,

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:06.650
walking,

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.840
eating, to the extraordinary,

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:10.270
inventing new technologies,

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:15.220
sculpting pottery and running marathons. To achieve these goals, our bodies depend 

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:16.620
on our organs, heart,

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:17.210
lungs,

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:17.590
brain,

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:18.180
et cetera,

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:19.340
working together.

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:20.700
Looking closer,

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:25.420
we see that each organ is made up of millions of individual cells working together.

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:26.620
Looking closer still,

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:29.090
we see that cells themselves are quite complex.

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:31.610
Every cell is essentially a tiny factory.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:32.720
Overall,

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:37.350
they break down chemical fuel for energy and produce proteins and other molecules.

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:41.090
If we take one final step closer to look inside of a cell,

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:45.810
we can see these activities occur in compartments called organelles, or little 

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:46.410
organs.

00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:47.870
When you think of a factory,

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.650
you probably think of a large multi-storey building that makes stuff.

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:54.610
The building has protective exterior walls,

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.470
which is like the cell membrane or plasma membrane,

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:02.600
but the factory also has doors with swipe card entry to let in the right people and 

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:03.240
supplies.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:04.500
Similarly,

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:05.610
the plasma membrane,

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:07.900
which is made up of lipids and proteins,

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:13.090
is a selectively permeable barrier that allows only certain molecules to come in or 

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:13.650
leave.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:16.840
This usually means good things like nutrients and hormones,

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:21.710
although sometimes viruses can trick the cells and enter by similar mechanisms.

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:24.850
Once the materials are through the plasma membrane,

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:28.010
some end up in another membrane bound organelle,

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:28.870
called an endosome,

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.210
or an internal delivery service.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:36.090
The endosome can deliver the material to many other organelles that we will learn about 

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:36.850
soon.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:42.260
The first compartment inside of our cells that we'll visit today is the most well known organelle:

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:43.450
the nucleus.

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:47.850
This is the combined IT department and CEO headquarters.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:51.930
It is where critical information is stored and decisions are made.

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:56.610
Crammed inside of the nucleus of every single cell is two meters of 

00:01:56.610 --> 00:02:00.870
DNA, fibers that contain all of the information necessary for making your 

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:03.560
proteins and for cell replication.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:04.800
The nucleus,

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:07.290
like the plasma membrane and many other organelles,

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.100
is surrounded by a lipid membrane.

00:02:09.230 --> 00:02:09.830
However,

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:14.420
it has relatively big pores called nuclear pores that allow RNAs 

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:19.290
and some proteins to pass between the inside, the nucleoplasm, and the 

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:20.810
outside, the cytoplasm.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:25.750
The cytoplasm is the name for the entire space between the nucleus and the plasma membrane.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:28.850
Inside of the nucleus sits the nucleolis,

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:30.730
an organelle within an organelle.

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:31.830
The nucleolis,

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:34.950
which is an example of a non membrane bound organelle,

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:39.230
is involved in making ribosomes, particles that synthesize proteins.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.360
Now, let's look more at manufacturing.

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:48.690
Sitting just outside of the nucleus is the endoplasmic reticulum,

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:53.410
or ER, a network of sausage-like tubes extending through the 

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:57.930
cytoplasm, that is involved in making proteins and lipids for cell 

00:02:57.930 --> 00:02:58.560
membranes.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:03.280
Once these materials are made in the ER,

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:08.160
they are sorted, packaged and delivered to the cell's shipping department,

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:09.550
the Golgi apparatus.

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:15.360
The Golgi apparatus repackages these proteins in small shipping containers called 

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:20.090
vesicles that get delivered to various other organelles and the plasma 

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:20.630
membrane.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:22.840
How does this delivery occur?

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:27.820
Analogous to the way in which packages can be taken around a factory along 

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:32.620
hallways, vesicles are transported by motor proteins along long 

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:36.440
protein fibres called microtubules and actin filaments,

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.990
which make up the cell's cytoskeleton.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.020
In addition to making new products,

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:44.930
cells also have to get rid of expired products.

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:49.240
The main waste disposal department is an organelle called the lysosome,

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:54.280
which can degrade or recycle waste. Factories need an energy source to 

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:59.200
keep all of this machinery working and so do cells. The food that we eat--

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:04.153
fat, carbohydrates and sugars-- is broken down by the cell and ultimately 

00:04:04.153 --> 00:04:08.723
needs to be converted to adenosine triphosphate, or ATP, for energy.

00:04:08.973 --> 00:04:10.553
Let's take fat as an example.

00:04:10.823 --> 00:04:13.993
The breakdown of fat is facilitated by peroxisomes,

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:19.043
which pass the products to mitochondria. A mitochondrian is the power plant that 

00:04:19.043 --> 00:04:23.323
takes a fuel derived from fat and converts it into ATP,

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:27.623
the main energy currency that cells use for their own activities.

00:04:28.613 --> 00:04:29.863
As our tour concludes,

00:04:29.873 --> 00:04:34.863
I hope that you can appreciate that the cell is a complex environment with many parts 

00:04:34.863 --> 00:04:37.153
working together to keep our bodies going.

00:04:37.933 --> 00:04:39.253
Thus not surprisingly,

00:04:39.263 --> 00:04:44.083
individuals with genetic disorders in one of the cells organelles often have 

00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:45.213
severe disease.

00:04:45.703 --> 00:04:49.293
As scientists learn more about the intricate work of organelles and health,

00:04:49.483 --> 00:04:50.753
stress and disease,

00:04:51.083 --> 00:04:54.933
new therapies to combat these disorders may become possible,

00:04:55.283 --> 00:05:00.073
and we can look forward to understanding more about how these tiny organelles make 

00:05:00.073 --> 00:05:00.713
our cells tick. 

